[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Web3 Development & Testing: Foundry, Hardhat, and Formal Verification"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building secure and robust decentralized applications requires modern tooling. In this tutorial, we will explore the dual-framework approach using Foundry and Hardhat, and how to mathematically prove smart contract correctness using Formal Verification."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Setting up the Development Environment"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Developers often leverage Hardhat for flexible JavaScript/TypeScript scripting and Foundry for high-performance Solidity-native testing. Initialize a hybrid project structure to get the best of both ecosystems."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"mkdir web3-secure-project && cd web3-secure-project
npx hardhat init
forge init --no-git"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Writing High-Performance Tests with Foundry"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Foundry allows you to write tests entirely in Solidity. It uses fuzz testing out of the box to discover edge cases that traditional unit tests miss."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.20;
import "forge-std/Test.sol";
import "../src/Vault.sol";

contract VaultTest is Test {
    Vault vault;
    function setUp() public {
        vault = new Vault();
    }
    function testFuzz_Deposit(uint256 amount) public {
        vm.assume(amount > 0 && amount < type(uint128).max);
        vm.deal(address(this), amount);
        vault.deposit{value: amount}();
        assertEq(vault.balanceOf(address(this)), amount);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Deployment and Automation with Hardhat"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Foundry excels at testing, Hardhat offers an expansive ecosystem of plugins for deployment scripts, verification, and task automation. Configure your hardhat.config.js to integrate network providers securely."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"require("@nomicfoundation/hardhat-toolbox");

module.exports = {
  solidity: "0.8.20",
  networks: {
    sepolia: {
      url: process.env.SEPOLIA_URL || "",
      accounts: [process.env.PRIVATE_KEY || ""]
    }
  }
};"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Introduction to Formal Verification"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Testing shows the presence of bugs, not their absence. Formal Verification uses mathematical models to prove that a smart contract fulfills its specification under all possible inputs and states. Tools like Certora or Halmos are commonly used for EVM bytecode verification."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write invariants specifying what must always be true (e.g., total supply equals the sum of all balances)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use symbolic execution to explore every execution path mathematically."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Detect subtle reentrancy or overflow vulnerabilities before deployment."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example Certora Invariant Rule
rule totalSupplyCheck() {
    mathint total = 0;
    foreach (address a in accounts) {
        total = total + balanceOf(a);
    }
    assert total == totalSupply(), "Supply mismatch!";
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Foundry for fast fuzz testing, Hardhat for versatile deployment pipelines, and Formal Verification for mathematical guarantees, you establish a production-grade Web3 development and testing pipeline."}]
